WEBVTT

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.460
Portraits of a vast, rugged, treacherous continent.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.850
They portray ships and men, machines and ice.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:30.000
A composite portrait of a continent which has challenged man since first he could sail beyond the limits.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:48.170
of his horizon, Antarctica, for the seas are as much a part of Antarctica as her highest

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:48.720
mountains.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.440
The first obstacles to exploration are products of the sea.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.740
Surrounded by three mighty oceans, the continent's only approaches are over the sea.

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:13.960
And finally, many of our greatest scientific rewards are coming from these seas.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:27.120
Not only the sea, but the air too, plays a vital part in understanding the Antarctic.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.000
For here, in this cold, unresolved...

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.200
existing medium, a modern explorer can transport himself over the continent efficiently and

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:45.400
economically. The helicopter uses the Antarctic airspace in its role as a modern-day pathfinder,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:55.640
transporting scientists into the field, as well as searching out leads in the ice, sea,

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:00.000
the air, and of course, the land.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:06.810
according to greek mythology was the gateway to the underworld a fitting name for this still active

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:14.960
volcano landmark in the rugged splendor of antarctica's vast reaches from the majesty of

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:21.550
mighty mountain ranges to these rocky crags guarding its coastline antarctica's land mass

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.430
sprawls over more than five million square miles

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:45.420
Beautiful, dangerous, hostile, yet the very elements that deny existence to man

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.680
provide a comfortable background for a handful of the local gentry.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:29.380
But the portrait of the land is also a portrait of its people who are the men of Antarctica.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:39.890
They are a composite of many races, many nations, from Admiral Byrd and other well-known faces from the past to the unfamiliar faces of the present.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:55.380
In Antarctica today, the name is not the important thing.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:00.000
What is important is what a man is able to convey.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.550
tribute to the enormous job at hand. Each job is small, sometimes almost insignificant.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:17.220
The total of all their efforts spells conquest, a conquest that never ends, that goes on year

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:27.620
after year, adding new victories to the successes of the past. How does this conquest take shape?

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:58.580
Or is it a battle of men against the weather and the elements? It's both. A quest continually pursued on all three fronts of the continent, land, sea, and air. Thus, mastery of the land alone is not enough. All three environments must be considered, and their respective obstacles overcome.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.560
This, then, is the first obstacle.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:08.320
Billions of tons of ice girdle the continent in a belt 100 miles in width.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:29.190
This belt must be broken for the annual return of the scientific support ships.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:44.980
The Old Navy and Coast Guard icebreakers undertake the job of carving these annual sea lanes into the continent.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:55.440
Their spoon-shaped bow is designed to ride up on the ice pack and crush down with thousands of tons of force, opening the channels for the cargo ships.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:48.490
Following in the wake of the icebreakers, these thin-skinned cargo ships bring in the thousands of tons of supplies needed each year to support man's continuing conquest of Antarctica.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:55.710
This is the offloading pier, eight feet of coastal ice at McMurdo Sound.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:07.240
From this thin strip of ice, the sea bees transfer the annual supply of groceries and diesel oil to an inland staging point.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:37.850
specific cargoes will be earmarked for distribution to the Antarctic stations

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:45.380
far inland some will be hauled in some air drop but whatever the method it's a

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:55.130
long way from the farm to the breakfast table while this section of the coast has

00:07:55.130 --> 00:08:00.000
been charted and is a familiar landfall to the support ships there are other coasts

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:10.650
other waters that remain unknown. And it is here that the icebreakers perform their second role,

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:23.500
that of a gargantuan tool of exploration. After a year, the seagoing men of Antarctica

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:30.000
chart new coastlines, sound out new waters, and report to the world their day

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:30.930
by day finding.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.610
Many nations send their scientists to explore these unknown waters.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:49.280
The effort to unveil Antarctica has become an international challenge.

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:54.580
As these men gaze upon these virgin lands for the first time, another sound of conquest

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:55.600
is being heard.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:13.090
Scientific outposts on the interior of the continent, hundreds of miles from any coastline, must be resupplied by air.

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:28.020
C-130 Hercules, newest and most personal addition to the Navy's air fleet, provides an efficient lifeline between the isolated stations and the coastal support base.

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:51.060
Her turboprop engines and especially configured ski landing gear permit for the first time giant loads of up to 10 tons to be ski landed on the natural snow surface of the polar plateau.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:40.380
At the same time that the C-130 Hercules delivers bulk loads to the inland stations and camps,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:45.940
additional supplies are airdropped by the Air Force's C-124 Globemaster.

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:49.690
Before the advent of the Navy's Hercules,

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:53.680
virtually all supply of inland stations was accomplished in this manner.

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:21.890
Flying a precision mission is one thing, but getting your plane off the ground is another.

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:27.260
These are Jato bottles, often necessary as a shot in the arm during takeoffs from the

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:30.000
snow surface.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:50.450
firing at once equal an extra engine wide open. One drawback, however, cutting loose with a full Jato firing cycle is almost like using your tail assembly for a rocket launching pad. Taking off is the R4D, one of the original Antarctic workhorses in the air.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.800
to the use of skis, this type aircraft was the first to land at the South Pole.

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:09.380
Although South Pole landings are now commonplace,

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:15.020
it wasn't long ago that this operation presented a highly hazardous challenge to the Antarctic airmen.

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:24.280
Nothing is routine in sub-zero temperature, not even normal maintenance.

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:29.730
This engine decided to call it quits at the South Pole.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:37.220
800 miles from the nearest air facility. But all the crew asks for is a new engine with something to lift it up.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:42.410
Even if the work isn't routine, the can-do spirit certainly is.

00:13:42.770 --> 00:13:52.190
Tying in closely with the conquest of the air is the taming of the land itself.

00:13:52.350 --> 00:14:00.000
This is a section of the snowmiller, a Swiss-built milling machine being flown into the construction site of the new bird station.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:07.480
These are the giant claws

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.220
of the 20-ton mechanical mole,

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:11.030
a strange-looking craft

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:12.390
with an insatiable appetite

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:13.590
for hard-packed snow.

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:27.920
It's dinner two stories high.

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.000
The snowmiller carves a four-by-eight

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:34.610
foot trench, paving the way for an entirely new type of Antarctic construction.

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:09.010
After the snowmiller completes its rough cut, the sides of the trench receive a

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:21.300
final surfacing. New Bird Station, typical of the trend toward permanency, sees its

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:30.000
first prefabricated arch swung into place. As fast as the assembly's

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:35.760
are installed. New material is flown in on a fast-paced schedule by the versatile C-130s.

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:51.150
Airborne fleet presents a far cry from the sluggish tractor trains that supplied the

00:15:51.150 --> 00:16:00.000
original bird station construction five years earlier. These giant arches will house the final

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:04.960
and protect them from the crushing weight of the compact snow.

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:39.750
Tunnels finished, construction of the buildings and storage areas is completed.

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:49.350
New Bird, the under-snow station of the future, home of scientific effort in Marie Birdland, occupied today.

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:58.930
And at the same time, Navy men burrow under the snow, ground is broken at McMurdo Sound for the construction of Antarctica's first nuclear power station.

00:17:10.330 --> 00:17:16.670
fabricated units arriving by ship were assembled by Navy CVs in less than 75 days, providing

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:25.660
1,500 kilowatts of power. Permanency, too, in communications, as this 110-foot rhombic

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:30.000
antenna joins the complex communication and weather network across the land.

00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:41.810
No longer defended upon inadequate radio facilities, Antarctica's far-flung outposts are now tied together with the latest of today's electronic systems.

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:49.130
But conquest of the land is much more than a permanent foothold on the ice.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:18:00.000
Its sound can be heard far inland, as men of Antarctica take to their vehicles, such as this one, equipped with an electronic vehicle.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.920
crevasse detector. Always on the move, they search for the uncharted mountain or the safe

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:12.370
trail through the crevasse fields. Navigating a thousand miles of uncharted ice cap is no

00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:17.350
mean task, but such is the goal set for the eight to ten man parties of the scientific

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:23.610
traverses. Such treks keep the men isolated in the loneliness of bleak windswept hinterlands

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:25.090
for three to four months.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:39.970
But this privation has its own rewards, and to the scientist, it is the chance to pursue studies in his own field, compile first-hand records of geophysical phenomena.

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:48.490
But the compulsion to find answers is not always the paramount order of business.

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:59.690
Sometimes, academic progress is overshadowed by the simple need for survival.

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:13.790
When the initial fuel supply is nearly exhausted, 700 miles from the base,

00:19:13.790 --> 00:19:21.190
the advanced fuel cache, parachuted or airlifted weeks before to a preselected site, is a welcome discovery.

00:19:37.960 --> 00:19:49.630
less welcome is this food cache, Antarctica's version of a turnpike drive-in. Trouble can

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:55.940
strike just as fast on land as it can on sea or in the air. A quick survey proves out the

00:19:55.940 --> 00:20:00.000
worst of their fears. The snowcat has broken through a snow bridge,

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:08.930
a dangerous crevasse. As far as the Traverse Party is concerned, this giant crack in the

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:11.810
continent's ice cap might as well be bottomless.

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:55.960
fortitude and can-do spirit of the old breed of Antarctic Explorer are by no

00:20:55.960 --> 00:21:00.000
means forgotten assets

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:14.150
A modern polar scientist is just as capable of substituting steel cable for his slide drill when the situation demands.

00:21:36.110 --> 00:21:40.360
Another round won, and the expedition moves on.

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:43.630
The challenge is still present, still strong,

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:49.230
still treacherous, but man's patience is infinite.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:51.830
Not all the men of Antarctica conduct their search

00:21:51.830 --> 00:21:57.400
for science on the move.

00:21:57.400 --> 00:22:00.000
Some remain buried in the far-flung out

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.230
post throughout the long Antarctic winter, such as South Pole Station.

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:34.640
Here they gather data on a wide variety of geophysical subjects,

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:38.910
containing knowledge of our Earth from an analysis of ice cores

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:44.470
revealing hundreds of years of Antarctica's history or the drift of a radiosonde balloon.

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:51.370
- Antarctica, too, has its moments of relaxation.

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:53.010
They are altogether too few.

00:23:53.010 --> 00:23:55.800
They still afford some relief in the heavy schedule.

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:53.900
radio stations at each one of the outposts, Navy men and scientists are able

00:24:53.900 --> 00:24:57.620
to talk to their families at home throughout the lonely months of isolation.

00:25:39.310 --> 00:25:53.640
The sleeping continent is awakening, slowly at first, but with ever-gathering momentum.

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:55.410
With each new fact learned,

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:06.150
With each new mile of coastline charted, Antarctica comes more and more into focus.

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:12.560
A continent of untapped scientific secrets, concealing a vast food supply in her waters,

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:28.140
holding the key to the source of much of the world's weather, a checkpoint for great circle aerial navigation.

00:26:33.260 --> 00:26:42.050
this is the portrait of Antarctica the ever-changing portrait and as she gives

00:26:42.050 --> 00:26:53.830
up the secrets held in her waters in her skies and in her landmass man's stockpile

00:26:53.830 --> 00:27:00.000
of knowledge grows larger for the sum of Antarctica's secrets is but one piece

00:27:00.160 --> 00:27:05.030
in the greater puzzle that challenges man on all corners of the universe.
